+### Burn after reading
+
+`POST /pastes` accepts an optional `burn_after_reading` boolean. When `true`, the snippet's first
+successful read is its last: the record is removed durably *before* the response is written, so it
+is served exactly once even when many callers read it at the same moment, and a restart cannot
+bring it back. A caller that abandons the connection mid-response still consumes it.
+
+```bash
+curl -s -X POST 127.0.0.1:8765/pastes \
+  -d '{"content": "read me once", "burn_after_reading": true}'
+curl -s 127.0.0.1:8765/pastes/$ID        # 200, the snippet
+curl -s 127.0.0.1:8765/pastes/$ID        # 404, permanently
+```
+
+Absent means `false`. The field must be a JSON boolean — `"true"`, `1`, `0`, `[]`, `{}`, and `null`
+are each a `400`; **absence, not `null`, is how a caller declines**.
+
+An unread one-time snippet still deletes normally with its token, and still expires on schedule.
+Expiry, damaged storage, and a refused password each leave it unconsumed.
+
+### Password protection
+
+`POST /pastes` accepts an optional `password` string of **1 to 128 characters** — counted in
+characters, not encoded bytes. Reading the snippet then requires the password in an
+`X-Paste-Password` header, whose bytes are interpreted as UTF-8.
+
+```bash
+curl -s -X POST 127.0.0.1:8765/pastes \
+  -d '{"content": "the secret", "password": "correct horse"}'
+curl -s 127.0.0.1:8765/pastes/$ID -H "X-Paste-Password: correct horse"   # 200
+curl -s 127.0.0.1:8765/pastes/$ID                                        # 403
+```
+
+Absent means unprotected. The field must be a string in range — `""`, 129 characters, a number, a
+boolean, `[]`, `{}`, and `null` are each a `400`.
+
+A read of a protected snippet is `403` with `{"error": "missing or invalid X-Paste-Password"}` when
+the header is absent, empty, wrong, carries bytes that are not valid UTF-8, or is **given more than
+once** — a correct password in a second copy of the header opens nothing, and no occurrence is
+selected. Matching is exact: nothing is trimmed, case-folded, or Unicode-normalised. A refusal
+leaves the snippet readable to whoever does have the password, and never consumes a one-time one.
+
+`404` outranks `403`: an unknown, expired, deleted, or already-consumed identifier is a `404`
+whatever password is presented. On an unprotected snippet the header is not inspected at all.
+
+Deletion needs only `X-Delete-Token`; the reading password neither substitutes for it nor is
+required alongside it. One verification is deliberately costly — 10–200 ms, calibrated to the
+serving host at startup — and does not delay requests about other snippets.
+
+### Reported flags
+
+`GET /pastes/<id>` reports `burn_after_reading` and `protected` as booleans that are always present
+and never `null`, so a caller can tell how a snippet is handled before relying on it. Both are
+computed from the record; neither discloses the password. A snippet stored by an earlier version of
+the service, which recorded neither option, reports `false` for both.
+
